.TH CTPOOL_TIMED_SUBMIT_FUTURE 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_timed_submit_future \- submit a future task with a relative timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ccol_retval_t ctpool_timed_submit_future(ctpool pool,
                                         void *(*fn)(void *),
                                         void *arg,
                                         uint64_t timeout_us,
                                         ctpool_future **out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a future and submits it to the pool, and on a bounded queue
waits at most timeout_us microseconds for a free slot, counted from
the call. The timeout is turned into an absolute deadline on
CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so a change to the system's wall clock (by an
administrator, by NTP, or when a virtual machine resumes) makes the
wait neither longer nor shorter.

A timeout_us of 0 makes the call the same as ctpool_try_submit_future,
which doesn't wait.

A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, waits as
long as the queue stays full: the deadline saturates at the latest
time that can be represented and never wraps into the past.

A call from a task running on this same pool, or from the on_complete
callback of that task, never waits while a bounded queue is at
capacity, because only the workers of this pool can free a slot and
the caller is one of them. It returns ccol_container_full at once,
whatever timeout_us says.

On success *out is set to a new ctpool_future handle with a reference
count of 2: the caller holds one reference, and the queued task holds
the other. The caller must call ctpool_future_free exactly once when
it is done.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool        Thread pool handle
fn          Task function returning void* (must not be NULL)
arg         The library gives it to fn (may be NULL)
timeout_us  Longest time to wait, in microseconds (0 means do not wait)
out         Receives the future handle on success. The function sets it
            to NULL on a failure (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            - task enqueued; *out set to the future handle.
ccol_invalid_args       - pool is CTPOOL_NULL or a stale/already-
                          destroyed handle, or fn or out is NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory  - future or task allocation failed.
ccol_not_permitted      - pool is shutting down.
ccol_container_full     - the queue is full and timeout_us is 0, or
                          the caller is a task of this pool (see
                          above).
ccol_timed_out          - timeout elapsed before a slot became free.
ccol_unexpected_failure - clock_gettime or pthread_cond_timedwait
                          returned an unexpected error.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_try_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_future_get (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
